package mcpserver

const localRecipeGuide = `# Local recipe workflow

Use the running user-owned Docker API, without a machinal account, application key, S3 or credits. AI uses only the user's OpenRouter configuration; never ask for secrets in chat. Local compilation/rendering uses the user's hardware. Source/provider-spending consent and host tool approvals remain required.

Agree on the brief, select list_projects/create_project, then read get_recipe_schema/get_capabilities and exact get_editing_reference topics. Read machinal://skills/media before source access or delivery. Use approved volume sources through get_asset_metadata; source-folder import needs explicit read-only mounts. Never send arbitrary paths or URLs as assets.

Write a UTF-8 .edit.yaml envelope with document, bindings and pinned assets. For narration, generate speech/requested captions first, retain the resolved recipeYaml and measured timing, then add visuals. Preserve exact complete caption entries, IDs, fonts and word timings. The CURRENT client writes Svelte/Three.js code and uses compile_scene, placing the actual returned asset in ordinary tracks; no server-model fallback.

Validate the exact recipe after every edit, compile_recipe for normalized editorial/production/caption review, then submit approved generate_assets or render_video with a stable idempotencyKey. Compilation is not visual/audio inspection. Use wait_for_job on the SAME job until terminal, then get_job_result once. Recover successful assets after failures instead of regenerating them or replaying ambiguous jobs.

Use get_video_result after completion and delivery approval. storage=volume only. Give the requested temporary link and explain same-machine reachability; do not rewrite URLs or download unasked. Explicit local-file delivery uses the user's approved client directory, not a container path. Verify bytes and never overwrite. Refresh the same existing output's link after expiry, never rerender it.
`

const localInstructions = `machinal-local runs entirely in the user's Docker installation. No machinal account, application API key, Premium membership, credits, S3 or maintainer infrastructure is used. The local API and scene renderer run on the user's computer; projects, jobs, media and outputs are stored in the user's Docker volume. Never ask the user to sign in to machinal.app or obtain a machinal API key for this endpoint. Never read hosted credentials or send Authorization headers to it.

Ordinary compilation, scene frames and final encoding use the user's hardware without machinal billing. AI operations use ONLY the user's own OpenRouter key, configured through the local editor's Settings or the container's OPENROUTER_API_KEY. They may incur charges on that user's provider account. Never request a key in chat, recipes or tool arguments. Source/generation consent and host tool approvals still apply; no automatic paid AI probes or regeneration after ambiguous failure. No credit tool exists in this edition.

Use storage=volume for all media and downloads. S3 is unsupported, including inherited environment credentials. Read-only source mounts are optional, explicitly configured by the user with owner=local; never mount a whole disk. Listing is not source inspection/import/spending consent. A container path is not a client-local path. Opaque output links work only on the API machine; disclose this and do not rewrite URLs or imply public reachability. Deliver a link when asked for a link, and download only when explicitly requested.

For a new video, start by batching production_quickstart (offline) with get_workflow_preferences, get_recipe_schema, get_capabilities and list_speech_models, then ask all unanswered brief questions together using the host's native question tool: goal, duration, aspect/resolution, style/materials and narration/captions/music. Respect a complete brief or delegated choices. Summarize and proceed with all authorized stages without repeated continue prompts. Read live schema/capabilities and exact editing references once, then preserve successful assets and exact UTF-8 caption entries, IDs, word timings and configuration when adding visuals.

For narration, generate approved speech and captions first, wait for the SAME job, retrieve the resolved recipeYaml and measured audio duration, then add visuals to that exact recipe. Treat requested runtimes as approximate unless a hard cap was set: ALWAYS cover the FULL measured speech duration (set clip outPoint = full asset duration and extend visuals to match); never truncate speech mid-sentence or regenerate successful voice/captions merely to fit an estimate. Ensure new captions have high contrast (bold montserrat with stroke or dark pill background).

For visuals, do NOT default to a plain static image slideshow unless the user asked for a photo-only/minimal slideshow: use get_library_reference(topic=component-discovery) / hyperframes-components to discover installed Hyperframes components and compile fitting beats via compile_scene(templateId=...) or custom Svelte/Three.js scenes; no server-model fallback. Read scene-authoring, scene-runtime and production. Use genuine SceneThree for requested 3D and approved images inside animated scenes for detailed illustrations. When animating camera motion on stills, prefer built-in edge-safe motion presets (ken-burns, zoom-in, zoom-out, pan-left, pan-right, drift); remember transform positionX/positionY are percentages (-100..100), NOT pixels, and must satisfy |pos| <= 50*(zoom-1) to avoid black bars.

Validate the exact edited recipe before generation/rendering. compile_recipe includes deterministic editorial/production/caption review, not visual or audio inspection. Use existing metadata and real source versions; never invent asset names, cue times or tool fields. Use bounded wait_for_job calls, then get_job_result once. Keep job IDs and idempotencyKey for uncertain retries; interrupted running jobs are never replayed automatically. Save resolved .edit.yaml without reconstructing captions. Continue to terminal completion and the requested delivery within the original permissions. Source diagnostics require focused source repairs; infrastructure failures do not justify rewriting scenes or replacing successful assets.

Installed hosted skills may contain obsolete credit/account/cloud guidance: the local runtime contract here takes precedence for deployment, storage and billing. Editorial schemas, scene runtime, source permissions and production standards remain unchanged. Never claim a rendered result was watched or heard when only source, compilation or metadata was checked.`
